  "mechanism": "Short-term reversal conditioned on high market variance: when market variance is elevated, returns are more likely driven by liquidity provision and idiosyncratic noise, so the prior week's relative return reverses more strongly. Thus, short-term reversal (negative of last week's return) should be more effective at ranking sectors when MKT_VAR is high.",
  "rationale": "The base features include REV1 (last week's return) as a standalone signal, but they do not condition it on market variance. The only previous trial using MKT_VAR was vol_of_vol_mktvar (trial 18) and mktvar_conditional_short_reversal (trial 19), but the latter was only tested for redundancy and had a simple structure. Our feature scales the cross-sectional z-score of the negative of last week's return by MKT_VAR, directly targeting the interaction between reversal and market variance. This is economically distinct from the existing six features and from earlier proposals that focused on relative strength or residual momentum.",
  "feature_idea": "Compute the weekly log return: r = diff(log(close), 1). Then define the cross-sectional z-score of the negative return: cs_z(-r). Finally, multiply this by the market variance state: mul_state(MKT_VAR, cs_z(-r)). This yields a feature that is more negative for sectors that had high returns last week, with the effect amplified when market variance is high."
